# local-fitness environment overrides.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (in the project root) and fill in the values
# you actually need. `.env` is gitignored. The CLI auto-loads it at
# startup. Real shell-set vars take precedence — useful when running in
# Docker (compose injects vars; .env is ignored inside the image).
#
# Every variable below is OPTIONAL. Defaults work for the host CLI on
# macOS with Garmin creds in the Keychain.

# --- Timezone ---------------------------------------------------------
# CONTAINER DEPLOYMENTS: set this. A container with TZ unset runs UTC,
# while the host CLI uses the machine's real zone — so for part of every
# day the two disagree about what "today" is, and this app is full of
# date-anchored reads ("today", "the last complete day", as-of-yesterday
# streaks, every trailing trend window). Nothing errors; you just get a
# confident answer computed against the wrong day. Measured on the
# reference deployment at 19:59 local, where the container thought it was
# 00:59 the next day: stress read 17 (-46.9%) instead of 28 (-12.5%), and
# form read "fatigued" instead of "very fatigued" — opposite coaching
# calls off the same database. The morning brief is unaffected (UTC and
# Central share a date at that hour), which is exactly why it hides.
#
# The host CLI needs nothing here — it inherits the OS zone.
# See docs/deployment.md for the compose wiring and a verification step.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_TZ=America/Chicago

# --- Garmin Connect ---------------------------------------------------
# When BOTH are set, env wins over the macOS Keychain (host CLI default).
# Required for container deployments — Linux containers can't reach the
# host's Keychain. Leave unset on a Mac and use `fitness setup` instead.
# GARMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# GARMIN_PASSWORD=your-garmin-password
#
# Where the Garmin session token is cached so pulls reuse a login instead of
# a fresh SSO login every time (which trips Garmin's login rate limit → 429).
# OPTIONAL: the app defaults to `~/.garminconnect/garmin_tokens.json`, which
# works on a fresh clone with no setup. Read by this app's code
# (`daily._tokenstore_path()`) as an explicit override, and honored by the
# garminconnect library (a garminconnect fork, not garth). Containers set it
# to a bind-mounted path so the host and container share one token (see
# docs/deployment.md).
# GARMINTOKENS=/home/app/.garminconnect/garmin_tokens.json
#
# How recently (minutes) a successful pull must have completed for the
# sync_garmin_data MCP tool to short-circuit as "fresh" without touching
# Garmin. Guards the chat shape where a re-asked question re-syncs — 8 of 24
# recorded sync calls were pure repeats minutes apart. The scheduled CLI jobs
# (`fitness pull` / `brief` / `brief-email`) never short-circuit; this gates
# only the MCP tool, and force:true bypasses it per-call.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_SYNC_MIN_INTERVAL_MIN=10

# --- Paths ------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults are project-relative: ./data, ./briefings, ./data/user_notes.md.
# Override only if you bind-mount volumes (containers) or want the data
# to live elsewhere. Ignored on the host CLI when unset.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_DATA_DIR=/data
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEFINGS_DIR=/briefings
# LOCAL_FITNESS_NOTES_PATH=/data/user_notes.md
#
# Where the file-writing MCP tools write PDF/PNG output:
# generate_brief_report and workout_report_card (stdio-only — a remote
# /mcp/ caller would get a container-internal path it can't retrieve) and
# chart's format="png" (both transports; it returns the PNG as an inline
# MCP image block, the saved path is a bonus for local callers).
# DEFAULT (unset): a per-process tmp directory
# (tempfile.mkdtemp), auto-cleaned via atexit when the `fitness mcp-stdio`
# process exits, and the generated file is auto-opened (macOS `open`) —
# nothing to manage, nothing accumulates. Set this only if you want output
# to persist instead (no auto-cleanup; still auto-opened either way).
# LOCAL_FITNESS_REPORTS_DIR=/Users/yourname/fitness-reports

# --- PDF report rendering (generate_brief_report, host CLI / macOS only) --
# WeasyPrint needs native Pango/Cairo/HarfBuzz libraries. On Linux (CI,
# Docker) `apt-get install` puts them on the normal dynamic-linker search
# path and nothing else is needed. On macOS, Homebrew installs them under
# /opt/homebrew/lib, which is NOT on the default dylib search path on Apple
# Silicon — WeasyPrint fails to import with
# `OSError: cannot load library 'libgobject-2.0-0'` until that path is
# exported. One-time setup on a Mac:
#   brew install pango
#   export DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH="$(brew --prefix)/lib"
# Put the export in your shell profile (or here, since local-fitness loads
# .env at startup) so `uv run fitness mcp-stdio` and pytest both pick it up.
# chart's png format alone (matplotlib only, no WeasyPrint) does not need this.
# DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/homebrew/lib

# --- Web server -------------------------------------------------------
# `fitness serve` defaults to 127.0.0.1 (localhost-only). Set to 0.0.0.0
# inside a container so Docker port-forwarding can reach it.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_HOST=0.0.0.0

# --- Display units ----------------------------------------------------
# Runner-facing distances/pace render in miles by default (mi, min/mi).
# Since 0.56.0 the LIST surfaces (query_workouts, daily_snapshot's recent
# workouts, plan progress) carry only the display form for the configured
# units — in miles mode the raw meters/sec-per-km twins are dropped from
# list rows (~25% of every payload was raw+formatted duplication). Detail
# surfaces (get_workout_detail, write echoes) keep raw + display side by
# side, and km mode keeps the raw trio everywhere (it IS the display form).
# Default is "miles".
# LOCAL_FITNESS_DISPLAY_UNITS=miles

# --- Brief reasoning effort (speed/quality lever) ---------------------
# Reasoning effort for the daily-brief composer: low | medium | high | max.
# Measured 2026-06-20: the brief's wall-clock is dominated by extended
# thinking; "low" cut a ~230s brief to ~82s while a blind A/B judge rated
# its content as good or better. Default is "low". Raise it if you want the
# model to deliberate more (slower). (The SDK's thinking budget_tokens knob
# is ignored on the Claude Code CLI path — effort is the working lever.)
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_EFFORT=low
#
# Brief composer architecture (agent/code separation). DEFAULT (unset) = V2: a
# deterministic planner pre-assembles the data + candidates and ONE toolless
# generator writes the prose (grounded, tested). Set to 0/false/no/off to roll
# back to the original V1 tool-driven monolith.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_V2=0   # uncomment to force the V1 fallback
#
# --- Brief stream resilience (2026-07-19) -----------------------------
# The nightly brief runs unattended; the Agent SDK stream occasionally dies
# (idle-out or subprocess crash). Defaults: declare a run dead after 120s
# with no stream message (0 disables the watchdog), make 3 total attempts,
# pause 20s between attempts. Normally leave all three alone.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S=120
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_RETRY_DELAY_S=20

# --- PDF/PNG brand theme (2026-07-19) ---------------------------------
# Every generated PDF (brief report) and chart PNG is styled by a brand
# theme. The built-in default is PRESS (warm cream paper, ink rules, one
# orange accent — the repo owner's brand). To use YOUR OWN branding,
# point this at a JSON file (data/ is gitignored — the natural home) with
# any subset of keys; they deep-merge over the default:
#   {
#     "colors":   {"paper": "#FFFFFF", "ink": "#111111",
#                  "dim": "#666666", "accent": "#0055FF", "rule": "#111111"},
#     "fonts":    {"mono_file": "~/path/to/YourMono.ttf"},
#     "identity": {"stamp": "AB", "brand_line": "MY FITNESS",
#                  "byline": "example.com/you"}
#   }
# A missing/broken file never breaks a render — defaults apply.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRAND_FILE=./data/brand.json

# --- Alt-model shadow-run (opencode CLI, diagnostic only) -----------------
# scripts/shadow_run.py / scripts/capture_baseline.py can shadow-run the
# brief generator against ANY model reachable via the opencode CLI (e.g.
# opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free), not just Claude — pass
# "opencode:<provider>/<model>" as --model. This is a shadow-run-only
# diagnostic path; it never touches the live production brief job.
#
# The opencode-CLI transport requires a TOOL-FREE opencode agent (never
# bash/edit/write access — this transport can send real prompt text,
# including your coaching notes, to a third-party model). A repo-local
# opencode.json/.jsonc is NOT auto-discovered by opencode, so add this agent
# to your OWN ~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc (merge into the existing
# "agent" map if you already have one):
#
#   {
#     "agent": {
#       "fitness-brief": {
#         "tools": {
#           "bash": false,
#           "edit": false,
#           "write": false,
#           "read": false,
#           "grep": false,
#           "glob": false,
#           "list": false,
#           "patch": false,
#           "webfetch": false
#         }
#       }
#     }
#   }
#
# Name of the tool-free opencode agent configured above. Default matches the
# snippet's agent name — only set this if you named your agent differently.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_OPENCODE_AGENT=fitness-brief

# --- Claude credentials (scheduled brief job) -------------------------
# The daily `fitness brief` job (and scripts/ab_brief.py) compose the
# brief via the Claude-bound headless agent, which authenticates with your
# Claude Max subscription token — no per-token API billing. The web server
# itself runs NO Claude inference, so it does NOT need this; only the
# scheduled composer does. The CLI auto-loads it from this .env, including
# under the minimal launchd environment (see ops/).
#
# Get it with the Claude Code CLI:  claude setup-token
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=paste-your-claude-oauth-token-here

# --- API auth ---------------------------------------------------------
# Bearer token gating /mcp/ (and every other non-public path). Without it
# set, /mcp/ exposes all wellness data unauthenticated. The server REFUSES
# to start on a non-loopback host without this set — loopback (127.0.0.1)
# keeps working without it for host-CLI dev convenience.
#
# Generate a fresh token:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#
# Then configure it as the Authorization: Bearer header in each MCP
# client's server config (Claude Code/Desktop/Mobile, opencode, etc.).
# LOCAL_FITNESS_API_TOKEN=paste-the-generated-token-here

# --- MCP server (interactive Claude access to your fitness data) ----------
# The /mcp/ endpoint exposes the fitness tools to your Claude Code / Desktop
# sessions over streamable-HTTP, behind LOCAL_FITNESS_API_TOKEN. The MCP
# transport's DNS-rebinding guard rejects any Host not in this allowlist
# (returns 421), so it MUST include the host you serve at. Comma-separated.
# Default (works for a fresh clone on loopback): 127.0.0.1,localhost
# Add any non-loopback host you serve at, e.g. an internal hostname behind a
# reverse proxy (fitness.home.local below is just an EXAMPLE override, not a
# default).
# LOCAL_FITNESS_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=fitness.home.local,127.0.0.1,localhost

# --- Grading / projection behavior (coaching preferences) ----------------
# How the training plan is graded and projected. Every default equals the
# historical hardcoded value, so a fresh clone behaves identically. These can
# also be set live (no restart) in the SQLite settings table via
#   uv run fitness config set <key> <value>
# When both are set, the DB value (config set) overrides the env var here.
#
# Do recovery WALKS satisfy an easy/recovery prescription? (long/tempo/
# interval/race always require running.) Default true.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COUNT_WALKS_EASY=true
#
# Include walking distance in the weekly-mileage rollup? Default false
# (weekly mileage is a running-volume metric).
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COUNT_WALKS_MILEAGE=false
#
# Grade-band fractions: actual/target >= done => "done"; >= partial =>
# "partial"; below => "missed". Must satisfy 0 <= partial <= done <= 1, else
# BOTH revert to defaults. Defaults 0.80 / 0.40.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_GRADE_DONE_FRACTION=0.80
# LOCAL_FITNESS_GRADE_PARTIAL_FRACTION=0.40
#
# Lookback window (days) for the "best recent effort" that drives the
# projected finish. Clamped to [1, 3650]; nonsense values fall back. Default 120.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_RIEGEL_LOOKBACK_DAYS=120

# --- Coach tone profile (how the brief talks) ----------------------------
# Who the coach is talking to. Used in every prompt the coach speaks through:
# the brief, chat, the PDF coaching line and the report-card read. Defaults to
# a generic "the user" — a real name is never a default in tracked code.
# Settable live via `uv run fitness config set user_name <name>`; a DB value
# overrides this env var.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_USER_NAME=
#
# Selectable coaching voice. One of: adaptive (default — today's behavior),
# supportive (upbeat), neutral (factual), hardass (relentless). Settable live
# via `uv run fitness config set coach_profile <name>`; a DB value overrides
# this env var. Default: hardass (0.31.0) — the accountability-mirror coach is
# the shipped identity; set adaptive/supportive/neutral to opt out.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_PROFILE=hardass
#
# Per-dial overrides (default = the selected profile's own value). These are
# GLOBAL — they apply to whichever profile is selected, so unset one when
# switching profiles if you want the new profile's native value. Dials clamp to
# 0-10; thresholds to 0.0-1.20. (The commented values below are adaptive's
# native dials; the hardass default runs 9/1/10 with 1.00/1.05 thresholds.)
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_HARSHNESS=6
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_WARMTH=6
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_PUSH=7
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_ROAST_THRESHOLD=0.85
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_PRAISE_THRESHOLD=0.95
#
# Tunable personality spec (0.31.0): the coach's personality is edited
# conversationally via the get/update_coach_personality MCP tools and stored
# in the DB (settings key coach_personality_spec); the profile .md files are
# only the first-run seeds. Set to 0 to IGNORE a stored spec and fall back to
# the shipped profile files (instant rollback of a bad tune — nothing is
# deleted). Default: on.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_SPEC=1
#
# Coach memory (0.30.0): a deterministic relationship ledger (adherence/step
# streaks, observation patterns) plus a coach-written journal, injected into
# every prompt surface and written back by an auto-reflect step after each
# brief/report card. Set to 0 to disable BOTH the injection and the reflect
# writes (instant rollback; journal data is untouched). Default: on.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_COACH_MEMORY=1
#
# --- Evening brief email (0.51.0) -------------------------------------------
# The `fitness brief-email` job (launchd 19:00, backstop 20:00) pulls fresh
# Garmin data, REGENERATES the day's brief against it — overwriting the morning
# one, which by evening is missing the day's training — and emails it as a
# PRESS-styled HTML report with the chart PNGs attached inline.
#
# Required to send. A Gmail app password (16 chars, from
# myaccount.google.com/apppasswords — the account needs 2FA on). Spaces in the
# pasted value are stripped. Without it the job exits 2 and sends nothing.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_PASSWORD=abcdefghijklmnop
#
# The account that sends the mail. Required alongside the password.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
#
# Where the brief is delivered. Comma-separated for several recipients.
# Defaults to LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_USER (i.e. mail yourself).
#
# This is the ENV layer only. Recipients resolve DB setting > env > default,
# and the DB layer is what the update_brief_email_settings MCP tool writes —
# so "also send it to my work address" is a sentence, not a file edit, and a
# conversational change silently outranks whatever is set here.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_EMAIL_TO=you@gmail.com
#
# Nightly-send kill switch, same DB > env > default precedence (default: on).
# Prefer `update_brief_email_settings(enabled=false)` over setting this —
# a DB value set that way will override this line.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_EMAIL_ENABLED=1
#
# Envelope sender, if it differs from the authenticating account.
# Defaults to LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_USER.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_BRIEF_EMAIL_FROM=you@gmail.com
#
# Any SMTP provider works — Gmail's values are only the defaults. Port 465
# uses implicit TLS; anything else (587, 2525) connects and upgrades via
# STARTTLS. Credentials never cross an unupgraded socket.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# LOCAL_FITNESS_SMTP_PORT=465
#
# --- Google Calendar: plan sync (0.52.0, reconciler since 0.53.0) -----------
# The `fitness plan-calendar` job (launchd 19:05, backstop 20:05) makes Google
# Calendar EQUAL the active training plan from today through its last day —
# a full reconcile (create, update, and delete) of the app's own all-day
# events, never an append-only write. The four plan-write MCP tools run the
# same reconcile after each edit. No active plan, or nothing remaining,
# writes nothing and exits 0.
#
# One-time setup — full walkthrough in docs/google-calendar.md:
#   1. Google Cloud project → enable the Google Calendar API
#   2. Create an OAuth client of type "Desktop app"
#   3. Set the consent screen to "In production". A Testing-mode app has its
#      refresh tokens EXPIRED BY GOOGLE every 7 days, so the job would die
#      weekly and look like a new bug each time.
#   4. Put the client id/secret below, then run `uv run fitness calendar-auth`
#      and paste the refresh token it prints.
#
# LOCAL_FITNESS_GCAL_CLIENT_ID=1234567890-abcdefg.apps.googleusercontent.com
# LOCAL_FITNESS_GCAL_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-example
# LOCAL_FITNESS_GCAL_REFRESH_TOKEN=1//0eExampleRefreshToken
#
# Which calendar to write to. 'primary' (the default) is the authenticated
# account's own calendar. This is the ENV layer only — it resolves DB setting >
# env > default, and the DB layer is what the update_plan_calendar_settings MCP
# tool writes, so a conversational change silently outranks this line.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_PLAN_CALENDAR_ID=primary
#
# Kill switch, same DB > env > default precedence (default: on). Prefer
# `update_plan_calendar_settings(enabled=false)` over setting this — a DB value
# set that way will override this line. Note that "on" writes nothing until the
# OAuth values above exist; the credential is the real gate.
# LOCAL_FITNESS_PLAN_CALENDAR_ENABLED=1
